    <section xml:id="sec-variability-journeys">
        <title>The Same Journey, Every Day Different</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                In August 2026, on a Friday evening, I was leaving Nairobi's CBD
                to catch a late flight home to London.
                I had a flight at 11:40pm and ordered an Uber around 7:30pm.
                The first driver appeared, then cancelled while I was outside
                looking for him.
                I had lost wifi and could not see what was happening.
                The second driver agreed to pick me up from a hotel across the
                road.
                I waited twenty minutes on that pavement, with cars around me
                moving slowly, and could not see the driver anywhere.
                I eventually walked to a nearby café to get wifi.
                In the cafe, I cancelled the second driver, and ordered a third
                from there.
                At least the café was slightly away from the worst of the
                gridlock.
            </p>

            <p>
                The app said the third taxi was ten minutes.
                They took over an hour.
                The app had estimated ten minutes — and I had believed it,
                because I had no reason not to.
                I sat watching the little car barely move, block after block, as
                the minutes ticked by and my flight window got smaller.
                I was not even in the taxi yet.
            </p>

            <p>
                I should also say: the café was not my idea.
                A worker outside noticed me standing on the pavement, clearly
                lost and increasingly frantic, and insisted I come in.
                She sat me down, got me connected to the wifi, and gave me a
                moment to think.
                Without that kindness I am not sure what I would have done.
                I caught the flight.
            </p>

            <p>
                I had not accounted for the time of day, the day of the week, or
                the particular chaos of Nairobi's CBD.
                But, anyone fromfrom Nairobi would have seen this coming
                immediately.
                Friday night, CBD, 7pm: that is a journey you plan hours around,
                or you do not make it.
                The traffic leaving the central business district on a Friday
                evening is not unpredictable.
                It is entirely predictable.
                It is just that I did not know to predict it.
                I had not accounted for the time of day, the day of the week, or
                the particular chaos of Nairobi's CBD.
            </p>

            <p>
                Imagine you had no information at all about how long a boda-boda
                ride across Nairobi would take.
                No knowledge of rush hour, no sense of which roads are usually
                busy, no awareness that Fridays are worse than Tuesdays.
                Every day, you would treat each journey as if it were identical
                to every other, and you would be wrong, repeatedly, in ways that
                cost you time, money, and patience.
                You would book the same amount of time for a 7am ride and a 5pm
                ride.
                You would be surprised, every single Friday afternoon, by the
                traffic.
                You would have no way to plan.
            </p>

            <p>
                Now imagine you know one thing: rush hour is slower.
                Suddenly you can plan.
                You leave earlier on the days that matter, and book less time
                for the quiet morning run.
                You may still be wrong sometimes (you cannot know about the
                accident on Ngong Road, or the blocked junction in town) but you
                are systematically less wrong than before.
                Knowing even one source of variation and accounting for it makes
                your predictions better.
                My mistake on that Friday evening was not unavoidable.
                It was a failure to account for something that was entirely
                knowable.
            </p>

            <p>
                This is what statisticians mean by the three types of
                variability.
                Some variation is <term>accounted-for variability</term>: you
                know what causes it, you have measured it, and you can use it to
                make better predictions.
                Time of day is exactly this — and it is exactly what I failed to
                account for on that Friday evening in August.
                Once you know it is 5pm on a Friday in Nairobi's CBD, you
                already know something useful about the journey ahead.
                Some variation is <term>unaccounted-for variability</term>:
                other things affect the journey — accidents, roadworks, the
                school term starting — but you have not measured them and cannot
                include them in your predictions.
                They are real causes of variation, making your predictions less
                reliable even when you have accounted for everything else you
                can.
                And some variation is just <term>natural variability</term>: the
                boda-boda rider caught a green light, or took a slightly
                different line through the roundabout, or the gap in traffic
                opened a few seconds earlier than usual.
                No amount of information would have predicted it.
                It simply happened.
            </p>

            <p>
                The problem is not the natural variability — any honest
                prediction has to live with it.
                The problem is mistaking unaccounted-for variation for natural
                variation.
                If you assume that everything you cannot account for is just
                noise, you will underestimate how wrong your predictions can be,
                and you will miss the fact that some of that wrongness has a
                cause that could, in principle, be found and understood.
                A boda-boda rider who thinks the variation in their journey
                times is just random bad luck will never think to check whether
                there is a pattern — whether certain roads are systematically
                worse, whether the school term changes things, whether the
                weather is a factor.
                Those patterns are real.
                Mistaking them for noise means never looking for them.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-traffic-variation">
            <title>Traffic Variation in Nairobi</title>

            <p>
                This variation has been measured.
                The
                <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/">TomTom Traffic Index</url>
                records journey times across Nairobi continuously, aggregating
                anonymised GPS data from millions of trips.
                It found that in 2025 a 10 kilometre drive in the city took an
                average of 26 minutes and 47 seconds.
                But that single number conceals an enormous range.
                At 4 in the morning, the same 10 kilometres takes around 18
                minutes.
                At 4 in the afternoon on a weekday, it takes around 37 minutes —
                more than twice as long.
            </p>

            <p>
                The visualisations by TomTom make the three types of variability
                visible in a way that numbers alone cannot.
            </p>

            <p>
                The first image below shows travel time across every hour of
                every day of the week, averaged across 2025.
                Each cell represents one two-hour slot on one day of the week;
                darker red means longer journey times, lighter pink means
                shorter.
                The pattern is immediately visible: weekday mornings and
                evenings are dark, weekend days are lighter, and the middle of
                the night is the lightest of all.
                This is <term>accounted-for variability</term> made visible: the
                variation that follows a predictable pattern, week after week,
                and that anyone who knows Nairobi already carries in their head.
                If I had looked at this chart before ordering my Uber on that
                Friday evening in August, I would have seen immediately that
                7:30pm on a Friday is one of the darker cells on the whole grid.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-heatmap-week">
                <caption>
                    Travel time heatmap for Nairobi across all hours and days of
                    the week, averaged across 2025.
                    Darker red indicates longer journey times; lighter pink
                    indicates shorter.
                    The pattern of weekday congestion, particularly the evening
                    peak from around 5pm to 7pm, is clearly visible, as is the
                    relative freedom of weekend mornings and nights.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025,
                    <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_heatmap_week.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A heatmap with days of the week (Monday to Sunday)
                            across the top and hours of the day (midnight to
                            11pm) down the left side.
                            Cells are coloured from light pink to dark red.
                            The darkest cells — indicating the longest journey
                            times — appear in the band from approximately 5pm to
                            7pm on weekdays, with Monday and Thursday appearing
                            particularly congested.
                            The 4am to 5am slot on Monday appears notably light.
                            Saturday and Sunday are substantially lighter
                            throughout, especially in the morning hours.
                            The pattern clearly shows the weekday rush hour
                            effect.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The next two images show where in Nairobi that congestion falls
                during the morning and evening rush hours — not just when, but
                where.
                Yellow hexagons indicate moderate congestion; orange and red
                indicate heavier delays.
                The CBD and the main arterial roads radiating outward from it
                are visibly darker than the outer suburbs.
                During the morning rush, the congestion concentrates in the city
                centre as commuters flow in; during the evening rush, it spreads
                more broadly as people try to get home.
                This spatial pattern is also displaying
                <term>accounted-for variability</term> — predictable,
                measurable, and useful for planning.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-map-morning">
                <caption>
                    Traffic conditions in Nairobi during the morning rush hour,
                    averaged across 2025.
                    Yellow indicates moderate congestion, orange and red
                    indicate heavier delays.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_map_morning.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A hexagonal heatmap overlaid on a dark map of
                            Nairobi.
                            The hexagons covering the central business district
                            and major roads leading into it are coloured orange
                            to red, indicating significant congestion.
                            The outer suburbs and peripheral areas are
                            predominantly yellow, indicating lighter congestion.
                            The pattern shows congestion concentrated in the
                            city centre and along main arterial routes during
                            the morning peak.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-map-evening">
                <caption>
                    Traffic conditions in Nairobi during the evening rush hour,
                    averaged across 2025.
                    The congestion spreads more broadly than in the morning,
                    with orange and red extending further into residential areas
                    as commuters make their way home.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_map_evening.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A hexagonal heatmap overlaid on a dark map of
                            Nairobi during the evening rush hour.
                            Compared to the morning map, the orange and red
                            hexagons extend further outward from the CBD into
                            surrounding residential neighbourhoods.
                            The overall impression is of broader, more
                            distributed congestion than the morning peak,
                            consistent with commuters dispersing in multiple
                            directions rather than converging on a single
                            centre.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                But even knowing the time of day and the location is not enough
                to predict any specific journey perfectly.
                The fourth image illustrates why.
                This is a screenshot I have just taken showing live congestion
                in Nairobi right now <fn>25th August 2026, 18:48 EAT</fn>,
                against the usual congestion level for the same time slot.
                The orange line is what is happening right now, the dotted
                yellow line is what typically happens at this time.
                On Monday morning, live congestion spiked to nearly 90 percent,
                well above the usual level of around 65 percent at that hour.
                On Tuesday afternoon, at the time this image was captured, live
                congestion was running at over 100 percent, higher than the
                typical level, and still climbing.
                Something was making that particular day worse than a typical
                Tuesday.
                Perhaps an accident, or roadworks, or nothing identifiable at
                all.
            </p>

            <p>
                This is <term>unaccounted-for variability</term> in action.
                TomTom's usual congestion line represents what the accounted-for
                variability predicts, the pattern that repeats week after week.
                The gap between that line and the live reading is the part that
                the pattern cannot account for.
                Some of that gap has a cause, something happened on that road on
                that day that pushed congestion above its normal level.
                And some of it is just <term>natural variability</term>: small
                fluctuations that remain even after everything predictable has
                been accounted for.
                The chart shows all three types of variability at once: the
                repeating pattern (accounted for), the unexplained spike above
                it (unaccounted for), and the jagged noise around both lines
                (natural).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-live-congestion">
                <caption>
                    Live versus usual congestion level in Nairobi across Monday
                    and Tuesday, with live speed shown below.
                    The orange line shows actual congestion; the dotted yellow
                    line shows the typical level for that time slot.
                    The gap between the two lines represents variation that the
                    usual pattern does not predict — unaccounted-for and natural
                    variability.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index, captured August 2026.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_live_congestion.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A line chart showing congestion level as a
                            percentage on the y-axis and time across Monday and
                            Tuesday on the x-axis.
                            An orange solid line shows live congestion and a
                            dotted yellow line shows the usual congestion level.
                            Both lines show peaks around 8am and 5pm on each
                            day.
                            On Monday afternoon, the orange live line spikes to
                            nearly 90 percent, substantially above the yellow
                            usual line of around 65 percent.
                            On Tuesday afternoon, the live line reaches over 100
                            percent and continues rising at the right edge of
                            the chart, labelled NOW, again running above the
                            usual level.
                            Below the congestion chart, a bar chart shows live
                            speed in km/h, with speeds dropping to around 10
                            km/h at peak congestion periods.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                <term>Source:</term> TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                Nairobi city profile.
                Available at:
                <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                Based on anonymised GPS data from drivers across the Nairobi
                metropolitan area.
                Live congestion chart captured August 2026.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-survey-variation">
            <title>Survey Data on Nairobi Commutes</title>

            <p>
                Survey data tells a similar story from a different angle.
                A 2013 World Bank survey of 14,580 households across 15 Kenyan
                cities found that the median commute time in Nairobi was 30
                minutes, but matatu commuters spent roughly twice as long
                travelling as walk commuters making similar trips in the same
                city.
                And an earlier survey from the same year found an average of 49
                minutes: more than 50 percent higher.
                Both surveys measured the same city in the same year.
                The difference reflects who was sampled, how questions were
                asked, and what counted as a commute, a reminder that
                variability is not just in the world, but in the measurement of
                it (Salon and Gulyani, 2019).
                Separate traffic modelling found vehicle speeds in Nairobi
                averaging just 8.3 km/h during the morning peak and 7.6 km/h
                during the evening peak, roughly walking pace.
                Similar patterns have been documented in Kampala, Uganda, where
                analysis of close to a million boda-boda trips found significant
                variation in travel speed across different periods of the day
                (Saddier, 2025).
            </p>

            <p>
                The three types of variability that this chapter is about are
                all visible in a single Nairobi commute.
                The time of day accounts for some of it — that is the part a
                good model can capture.
                An unknown accident or a burst water main explains more — that
                is unaccounted-for variability, real causes that were simply not
                measured.
                And pure randomness explains the rest — natural variability, the
                irreducible noise that no model can eliminate.
                Understanding what each type contributes, how to measure the
                total, and what to do about it: that is what this chapter is
                about.
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>Sources:</term> TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                Nairobi city profile.
                <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                Salon, D.
                and Gulyani, S.
                (2019).
                <q>Commuting in Urban Kenya: 
    Unpacking Travel Demand in Large and Small Kenyan Cities.</q>
                <em>Sustainability</em>, 11(14), 3823.
                <url href="https://doi.org/10.3390/su11143823"/>.
                CC BY 3.0.
                Saddier, S.
                (2025).
                <q>Are motorcycle taxis competing with collective 
    public transport? Analyzing the role of boda-bodas in Kampala's urban 
    mobility system.</q>
                <em>Research in Transportation Economics</em>, 111, 101562.
                <url href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.retrec.2025.101562"/>.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

